s, as they are the mobile charge carriers in most materials.\n3. Protons are bound within the atomic nucleus and cannot be transferred between materials under normal conditions.\n4. If fur were to give up protons, it would not be able to do so because protons are tightly bound in the nucleus.\n5. Fur cannot gain protons from the rod because fur is not a source of protons.\n6. Fur cannot give up protons to the rod because fur does not have protons to give up.\n7. Fur cannot give up protons to the fur itself because fur is not a source of protons.\n8. Fur cannot give up protons to the fur itself because fur is not a source of protons.\n9. Fur cannot give up protons to the fur itself because fur is not a source of protons.\n10. Fur cannot give up protons to the fur itself because fur is not a source of protons.\n11. Fur cannot give up protons to the fur itself because fur is not a source of protons.\n12. Fur cannot give up protons to the fur itself because fur is not a source of protons.\n13. Fur cannot give up protons to", "new_cot_probs": [0.0010886408854275942, 0.013262361288070679, 0.00024290861620102078, 0.05943777784705162], "cot_prob": [-2.2533304691314697], "cot_step_prob": [-6.409695625305176], "mechanistic_diagnostics": {"prompt_lengths": {"answer_prompt_tokens": 204, "removed_answer_prompt_tokens": 193, "step_token_count": 14}, "representation": {"step_answer_cosine_by_layer": [-0.0011003483086824417, 0.8502883315086365, 0.769051730632782, 0.7370433807373047, 0.7066924571990967, 0.6952239274978638, 0.6544580459594727, 0.616531491279602, 0.6515814065933228, 0.6402081847190857, 0.6322911381721497, 0.6533741354942322, 0.6361511945724487, 0.615082323551178, 0.6215978264808655, 0.5794352889060974, 0.5799931287765503, 0.5888721346855164, 0.6005632877349854, 0.6444218754768372, 0.6134496331214905, 0.6098942756652832, 0.6199080348014832, 0.6460217833518982, 0.6614749431610107, 0.7150018215179443, 0.7317447066307068, 0.7197529673576355, 0.7612473964691162, 0.77426743