{"id": "NYSEDREGENTS_2008_8_30", "question": "Part of the east coast of South America and the west coast of Africa have matching fossils within the same series of rock layers. This provides evidence that these two continents were once", "step_idx": 3, "options": ["1): separated by a much larger ocean", "2): joined together as one landmass", "3): located near the North Pole", "4): in a different hemisphere"], "correct": "B", "initial_cot": "1. The question mentions that there are matching fossils found in the same series of rock layers on the east coast of South America and the west coast of Africa.\n2. This indicates that these two continents must have had a similar environment and shared the same species of organisms at some point in time.\n3. The presence of matching fossils in the same rock layers suggests that these continents were once connected, allowing the same species to exist in both regions.\n4. The continents are now separated by the Atlantic Ocean, which formed after the breakup of the supercontinent Pangaea.", "initial_cot_probs": [1.6046869388830487e-09, 7.886666026024614e-06, 5.209656039895094e-10, 2.460867343234696e-10], "initial_probs": [2.097748534879429e-07, 0.00020301493350416422, 2.5054031738136473e-08, 1.5196039981901777e-08], "prediction": 1, "cot_prediction": 1, "adapter_id": "NYSEDREGENTS_2008_8_30_step_3", "global_job_index": 1287, "cot_step": "This indicates that these two continents must have had a similar environment and shared the same species of organisms at some point in time.", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "The question mentions that there are matching fossils found in the same series of rock layers on the east coast of South America and the west coast of Africa.", "2.", "This indicates that these two continents must have had a similar environment and shared the same species of organisms at some point in time.", "3.", "The presence of matching fossils in the same rock layers suggests that these continents were once connected, allowing th