0.0007560784579254687], [5.3224735893309116e-05, 0.0005722198402509093, 5.3224735893309116e-05, 0.17978613078594208], [0.04647349938750267, 0.00040207456913776696, 0.0006629088893532753, 0.00011519628606038168], [0.00039177233702503145, 0.0746585875749588, 4.129250010009855e-05, 8.741622150409967e-05], [0.09245147556066513, 0.0007998620276339352, 0.00017847334675025195, 0.00013899516488891095], [0.0010941687505692244, 0.0038190241903066635, 0.0002441420510876924, 0.20851166546344757], [0.00013644246791955084, 0.11652988940477371, 0.00028884870698675513, 0.0006114927236922085]], "new_cot": "1. The car is rolling on a level street.\n2. We want to find out what can make the car slow down and eventually make it stop.\n3. When something slows down and makes it stop moving, that means there must be a force acting opposite to the direction of motion.\n4. At this point, we can eliminate answers A and B:\n   (A) A force that pushes straight up is called the normal force. The normal force acts opposite to gravity, perpendicular to the surface, so it doesn't make the car slow down and eventually make it stop.\n   (B) A force that pushes straight down is gravity, which acts downward toward the center of Earth. Gravity causes a object to have weight, but it doesn't make a moving object slow down and eventually make it stop (unless there's no other force, like friction, to make it slow down).\n5. Now we're left with answers C and D:\n   (C) A force that pushes the same direction that the car is moving is called a force that makes the car speed up or stay moving. This isn't what we're looking for, since we want to find a force that makes the car slow down and eventually make it stop.\n   (D) A force that pushes the opposite direction that the car is moving is called friction. Friction is a force that", "new_cot_probs": [0.0019047388341277838, 0.0011552823707461357, 0.001483411993831396, 0.7684264183044434], "cot_prob": [-1.8650648593902588], "cot_step_prob": [-5.05286169052124], "