230402704095468, 1.2713721275758871e-07, 2.6914949557976797e-07]], "new_cot": "1. Ice can change Earth's surface through the process of glaciation.\n2. Glaciation involves the movement of glaciers, which are large masses of ice.\n3. As glaciers move, they can exert pressure on the Earth's surface.\n4. This pressure can cause rocks to crack and break apart.\n5. Glaciers can also erode the land by scraping and carving it.\n6. One of the most common landforms created by glacial erosion is a U-shaped valley.\n7. U-shaped valleys are formed when glaciers carve out a wide, U-shaped valley as they move downhill.\n8. Therefore, the two ways ice can change Earth's surface are by cracking a rock and making a U-shaped valley.", "new_cot_probs": [2.7514455723576248e-05, 6.343728653490643e-09, 1.0459040744592585e-08, 1.603945753991809e-09], "cot_prob": [-0.3532674014568329], "cot_step_prob": [-0.8512082099914551], "mechanistic_diagnostics": {"prompt_lengths": {"answer_prompt_tokens": 309, "removed_answer_prompt_tokens": 296, "step_token_count": 23}, "representation": {"step_answer_cosine_by_layer": [0.03972261771559715, 0.8693497180938721, 0.776574432849884, 0.4698708653450012, 0.46830570697784424, 0.5315691828727722, 0.5276212692260742, 0.539153516292572, 0.6951127648353577, 0.6920530796051025, 0.7012010216712952, 0.7133524417877197, 0.6898825168609619, 0.6661757230758667, 0.6811199188232422, 0.6320140361785889, 0.6234437823295593, 0.6325594186782837, 0.6480398178100586, 0.7047535181045532, 0.6719631552696228, 0.6682203412055969, 0.6754674911499023, 0.6983574628829956, 0.6999849677085876, 0.7527445554733276, 0.7682061791419983, 0.7761468291282654, 0.8124387860298157, 0.8253490924835205, 0.8396324515342712, 0.8562633395195007, 0.6729429960250854], "answer_removed_cosine_by_layer": [1.0, 0.9998988509178162, 0.9997940063476562, 0.9994853138923645, 0.9993492364883423, 0.9987472295761108, 0.9985153079032898, 0.9983804225921631, 0.9983011484146118, 0.998426616191864, 0.99833643436431